const e=[{community_id:0,name:"Foundation Models / LLMs",tagline:"Scaling, sparsity, and the reasoning frontier",description:"The map's largest and most central school spans pretraining, architecture, and reasoning for frontier LLMs, and it grew markedly from 29 Oral papers in 2025 to 40 in 2026. A dominant thread is Mixture-of-Experts efficiency and sparsity, from Jin's zero-computation experts (MoE++) to Nakamura's study of optimal MoE sparsity for reasoning. A second runs through evaluation integrity, where Dominguez-Olmedo shows that 'training on the test task' confounds emergence claims. The 2026 cohort leans harder into reasoning, long-context, state-space alternatives (Mamba-3, FlashRNN), and multimodal priors — Han demonstrates that text-only LLMs develop latent visual priors. Across both years, scaling laws, DPO/RLHF post-training, and test-time compute recur as organizing concerns, and bibliographic coupling ties 2025 MoE and multimodal work like Transfusion directly to their 2026 descendants.",methods:["Mixture-of-Experts routing","Scaling-law analysis","Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)","Test-time / inference-time compute","State-space & linear-attention architectures"],key_contribution:"It charts how frontier-model research consolidated around sparse expert architectures, reasoning-oriented post-training, and evaluation-aware scaling as the field moved from 2025 to 2026.",exemplars:[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348"},{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890"},{ay:"Han 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039"}]},{community_id:1,name:"Alignment, Safety & Interpretability",tagline:"Guardrails, watermarks, and opening the black box",description:"Nearly constant in size across the two cohorts (31 then 30 Oral papers), this school pairs safety engineering with mechanistic interpretability. One cluster probes how alignment is implemented and broken: Qi argues safety alignment is 'more than just a few tokens deep,' Zhou localizes safety to specific attention heads, and 2026 work exposes malicious fine-tuning via steganography and dormant adversarial behaviors. A parallel cluster advances interpretability — sparse autoencoders (Gao), sparse feature circuits (Marks), and 2026 temporal SAEs and chain-of-thought verification. Provenance and IP concerns run throughout, from copyright-protected generation to LLM fingerprinting and forgery-resistant signatures. Preference optimization for safety, via SafeDPO and semi-supervised preference optimization, links this school tightly to Foundation Models — the strongest edge in the entire map.",methods:["Sparse autoencoders & feature circuits","Safety-alignment fine-tuning","Watermarking & fingerprinting","Data attribution / influence functions","Preference optimization (DPO variants)"],key_contribution:"It shows safety and interpretability converging on a shared agenda — locating, auditing, and provably attributing model behavior — that held steady in volume even as the field's other priorities shifted.",exemplars:[{ay:"Qi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.05946"},{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.13708"},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20065"},{ay:"Gloaguen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.16723"},{ay:"Choi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.08069"}]},{community_id:2,name:"Science, Robotics & Systems",tagline:"Proteins, brains, robots, and PDEs",description:"This applications-heavy school gathers AI for the physical and life sciences, robotics, and systems, easing slightly from 26 to 23 Oral papers. Protein and molecular design is the backbone: Geffner's flow-based Proteina in 2025 gives way to 2026's consistency-model DCFold and atomistic binder design with test-time compute. Neuroscience is a second pillar — decoding visual stimuli from fMRI (Huang), topographic language models, and hippocampal spatial coding — while robotics contributes data-scaling laws for imitation learning (Lin) and diffusion-based planning. Physics-informed and PDE modeling recurs across both years, from fluid simulation via diffusion graph networks (Valencia) to physics-informed networks and Koopman representations. Coupling to Generative Models is very strong, reflecting how flow matching and diffusion have become the shared toolkit for scientific structure generation.",methods:["Flow matching & diffusion for structures","Graph neural networks","Imitation learning for manipulation","Physics-informed / PDE modeling","fMRI & neural population decoding"],key_contribution:"It captures how generative modeling — especially flow matching and diffusion — became the common engine for de novo protein design, robotic planning, and brain decoding across both cohorts.",exemplars:[{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.18647"},{ay:"Geffner 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00710"},{ay:"Huang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.16196"},{ay:"Zhang 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Valencia 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2504.02843"}]},{community_id:3,name:"Graphs, Probabilistic & Other Methods",tagline:"Expressivity, sampling, and causal structure",description:"A methods-focused school that contracted the most in relative terms (27 down to 21 Oral papers), spanning graph learning theory, probabilistic inference, sequence models, and causal discovery. Graph neural network expressivity is a signature thread — homomorphism expressivity of spectral-invariant GNNs (Gai), Hölder stability, and complete logical frameworks — increasingly joined in 2026 by graph foundation models and in-context graph alignment. Probabilistic and sampling methods form a second core: variational diffusion posterior sampling (MOUFAD), Gaussian processes, and optimal-transport approaches to speculative decoding (Thomas) and hyperparameter trajectory inference (Amad). Linear RNNs and state-space expressivity (Grazzi, oscillatory SSMs) bridge toward sequence modeling, while causal-discovery papers on selection bias and latent confounders persist across both years.",methods:["GNN expressivity analysis","Variational inference & posterior sampling","Optimal transport","State-space / linear RNN models","Causal structure discovery"],key_contribution:"It threads a rigor-first agenda — provable expressivity, principled sampling, and identifiable causal structure — that increasingly borrows the foundation-model vocabulary of pretraining and in-context learning.",exemplars:[{ay:"Grazzi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.12537"},{ay:"Gai 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00485"},{ay:"MOUFAD 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.09945"},{ay:"Thomas 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Amad 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2603.01771"}]},{community_id:4,name:"Generative Models",tagline:"Diffusion, flow, and the AR-diffusion merger",description:"One of the fastest-growing schools (19 to 25 Oral papers), centered on the machinery of diffusion, flow matching, and autoregressive generation. A defining 2025-to-2026 arc is the blurring of the AR/diffusion boundary: Arriola's Block Diffusion interpolates between the two, while 2026 work has diffusion language models that 'know the answer before decoding' and planner-aware path learning. Efficiency is pervasive — continuous-time consistency models (Lu), one-step distillation, parallel decoding for AR images (Zhang), and feature caching for diffusion transformers. Representation alignment for generation (Yu's REPA) is among the map's highest-degree hubs, and 2026 pushes into video and 3D scene generation, world models, and reward-aligned flows. Energy-based transformers (Gladstone) reintroduce System-2 verification into the generative stack.",methods:["Diffusion & score-based models","Flow matching","Consistency-model distillation","Autoregressive tokenization & decoding","Discrete / masked diffusion"],key_contribution:"It documents the convergence of autoregressive and diffusion generation and a relentless push toward few-step, representation-aligned samplers, expanding fastest into video, 3D, and world models.",exemplars:[{ay:"Yu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06940"},{ay:"Lu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11081"},{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01957"}]},{community_id:5,name:"Multimodal Applications",tagline:"Video, avatars, segmentation, and GUI agents",description:"Essentially flat year over year (20 then 21 Oral papers), this school applies large models to vision, audio, and interactive modalities. Audio-driven human animation is a persistent specialty — Loopy and CyberHost in 2025, cognitively-simulated avatars and expressive podcast TTS (VibeVoice) in 2026. Promptable segmentation (RMP-SAM, SAM 2, EmbodiedSAM) and 3D reconstruction via Gaussian splatting recur throughout. A growing agentic strand connects GUI grounding (Gou) to 2026 computer-use agents (ScaleCUA) and long-horizon multi-turn RL training. Efficiency for video LLMs via token merging (FlashVID) and multimodal retrieval round out the 2026 cohort. The school couples strongly to both Generative Models and Foundation Models, reflecting its reliance on diffusion backbones and MLLM reasoning.",methods:["Promptable segmentation (SAM-family)","Audio-driven video/avatar diffusion","3D Gaussian splatting & view synthesis","Visual token compression","GUI / computer-use agents"],key_contribution:"It tracks multimodal generation and perception maturing into interactive, agentic systems — from talking-avatar synthesis to computer-use agents — while holding steady in Oral volume.",exemplars:[{ay:"Fan 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.09967"},{ay:"Xu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2401.10228"},{ay:"Gou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.05243"},{ay:"Jiang 2026",doi:null}]},{community_id:6,name:"Learning Theory & Optimization",tagline:"Provable training, landscapes, and equilibria",description:"A theory-and-optimization school that expanded from 17 to 22 Oral papers, supplying the mathematical underpinnings for much of the rest of the map. Optimization contributions target training efficiency — accelerated gradient propagation along residual paths (Fagnou), subspace and low-rank training, and the matrix-sign methods behind the Muon optimizer (Amsel). Learning-theory work explains phenomena observed empirically elsewhere: the sharpening mechanism behind LLM self-improvement (Huang), when task vectors provably enable model editing (Li), length-generalization bounds, neural collapse, and feature-learning scaling laws. A distinct game-theoretic cluster studies equilibrium computation, min-max optimization, and online learning with ranking feedback (Liu). The 2026 cohort leans further into optimizer analysis, phase-retrieval dynamics, and black-box/quality-diversity optimization.",methods:["Convergence & generalization bounds","Convex / min-max optimization","Feature-learning & scaling-law theory","Low-rank / subspace training","Game-theoretic equilibrium computation"],key_contribution:"It provides the provable scaffolding — generalization bounds, optimizer analysis, and equilibrium theory — that formalizes the empirical behaviors the rest of the constellation reports.",exemplars:[{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Fagnou 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2501.17086"},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2504.10957"},{ay:"Tjanaka 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2601.01082"},{ay:"Liu 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2603.19221"}]},{community_id:7,name:"Datasets & Benchmarks",tagline:"Measuring agents, code, and conversation",description:"This school grew from 16 to 18 Oral papers and increasingly benchmarks capabilities rather than static datasets. The clearest 2025-to-2026 shift is toward agentic and interactive evaluation: BigCodeBench and MLE-bench in 2025 give way to MedAgentGym, Gaia2's asynchronous environments, SimuHome, and AstaBench's scientific-research suite in 2026. Text-to-SQL evolves from Spider 2.0 to interaction-centric BIRD-INTERACT, and code editing gets EditBench. Evaluation reliability is a recurring concern — reward-model benchmarking (RM-Bench), multi-turn degradation ('LLMs Get Lost'), and privacy auditing (Marek). Data-centric contributions include the ethically-sourced Common Corpus and dataset-bias retrospectives. Cybersecurity benchmarks (Cybench, CyberGym) span both years, underscoring a move toward measuring real-world, safety-relevant competence.",methods:["Agentic task environments","Code-generation benchmarks","LLM-as-judge evaluation","Multimodal & text-to-SQL benchmarks","Privacy & contamination auditing"],key_contribution:"It marks the field's pivot from static leaderboards to interactive, agentic, and reliability-focused evaluation that stresses models in realistic, multi-step settings.",exemplars:[{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.16184"},{ay:"Zhuo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.15877"},{ay:"Xia 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.10139"},{ay:"Xu 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.04405"},{ay:"Laban 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.06120"}]},{community_id:8,name:"Representation & Transfer Learning",tagline:"Forgetting, geometry, and continual adaptation",description:"A compact school that grew from 13 to 16 Oral papers, focused on how representations are learned, transferred, and preserved. Continual learning and catastrophic forgetting anchor both cohorts — function vectors for continual instruction tuning (Jiang), SD-LoRA, and 2026's Frobenius-isometry reinitialization (FIRE) for the stability-plasticity tradeoff. A representation-geometry thread studies latent-space dynamics as attractors (Fumero), conformal isometry of grid cells, and object-centric world models (Daniel). Self- and semi-supervised learning recurs via FixMatch analysis (Li), dense retriever pretraining (Revela), and contrastive vision-language studies of the modality gap. Identifiability and causal representation learning appear in both years. The school couples to Foundation Models through work tracing model lineage via functional representations (LLM DNA).",methods:["Continual learning & LoRA adaptation","Contrastive / self-supervised learning","Representation-geometry analysis","Object-centric world models","Causal representation identifiability"],key_contribution:"It concentrates on preserving and understanding learned representations — combating forgetting, characterizing latent geometry, and identifying generative factors — across supervised and self-supervised regimes.",exemplars:[{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.11019"},{ay:"Pal 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06912"},{ay:"Li 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11206"},{ay:"Fumero 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Han 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.08040"}]},{community_id:9,name:"Reinforcement Learning",tagline:"Skills, policies, and preference games",description:"The school that shrank most sharply, from 15 Oral papers in 2025 to just 8 in 2026, as much RLHF-flavored work migrated into the Foundation Models and Alignment clusters. What remains is comparatively classical RL: preference alignment framed as no-regret game play (Iterative Nash Policy Optimization), open-ended environment design (Kinetix), and skill discovery via mutual information or AI feedback (MISL, MaestroMotif). Generative policies are a live 2026 thread — mean-flow one-step action generation (Zhan) and exploratory diffusion for unsupervised RL — alongside zero-shot and behavior-foundation models (TD-JEPA, Optimistic Task Inference). Theory persists through the geometry of continuous-state RL (Tiwari) and linear Bellman completeness. The 2026 survivors increasingly borrow generative-model and representation machinery rather than standalone value learning.",methods:["Policy optimization & RLHF","Unsupervised skill discovery","Generative / flow-based policies","World models & model-based RL","Zero-shot behavior foundation models"],key_contribution:"It shows core reinforcement learning contracting as preference optimization diffused into other schools, leaving a residue of skill discovery, generative policies, and RL theory that increasingly leans on generative-model tools.",exemplars:[{ay:"Zhang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.00617"},{ay:"Matthews 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.23208"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.08021"},{ay:"Zhan 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.13810"},{ay:"Zhao 2026",doi:null}]}],i={title:"No Common Ground: Learning Theory Never Reaches Reinforcement Learning",statement:"Across both the 2025 and 2026 Oral cohorts, Learning Theory & Optimization and Reinforcement Learning are the two schools that barely share references. Theory work concentrates on convexity, loss landscapes, generalization bounds, and matrix-sign optimizers (Amsel 2025, 'The Polar Express'), while the RL cluster is dominated by empirical skill discovery, world models, and policy methods (Zheng 2024, 'Can a MISL Fly?'). The missing bridge is rigorous sample-complexity and convergence guarantees carried INTO sequential decision-making: even the equilibrium and online-learning papers that could connect them (Liu 2026, 'Online Learning and Equilibrium Computation with Ranking Feedback') sit on the theory side with no RL coupling. The two threads run parallel but disjoint through both conferences.",structural_evidence:"In the 10-community adjacency, the Learning Theory & Optimization to Reinforcement Learning edge carries a weight of 60.7 - the lowest of any inter-cluster pair, below the next-weakest links at 67.6 and 72.7. By contrast the strongest pair, Foundation Models / LLMs to Alignment, Safety & Interpretability, couples at 1580.8, over 25 times denser. The field pours shared references into the LLM-safety core while theory and RL remain a structural dead zone.",why_it_matters:"Reinforcement learning is now the engine of frontier reasoning - RLHF, RLVR, and test-time compute all run on it - yet the school that owns convergence and sample-complexity theory scarcely references the school that owns decision-making. The most consequential training procedures of 2026 therefore advance largely without theoretical guarantees. A field that cannot connect its optimization theory to its RL methods risks scaling recipes it can neither explain nor bound.",what_would_resolve_it:"A paper or benchmark that carries rigorous optimization and sample-complexity guarantees into modern RL-for-LLM settings would wire the clusters together. Concretely, extending the coverage-principle line (Chen 2025, 'The Coverage Principle: How Pre-Training Enables Post-Training') or linear-Bellman-completeness RL theory (Wu 2024, 'Computationally Efficient RL under Linear Bellman Completeness for Deterministic Dynamics') to RLHF and RLVR reasoning objectives, paired with a shared benchmark that scores convergence AND task reward. Applying the theory school's equilibrium-computation tools (Liu 2026, 'Online Learning and Equilibrium Computation with Ranking Feedback') to preference-based RLHF would connect the two schools directly."},a={claim:"From 2025 to 2026 the field's center of gravity shifted from designing autonomous agents toward building, scaling, and governing large reasoning models: frontier LLMs and their alignment scaffolding fused into a dense core, generative and optimization research reoriented to serve that core, and reinforcement learning survived mainly as a fine-tuning verb inside the LLM stack rather than as a standalone research school.",evidence:[{point:"Foundation Models / LLMs became the gravitational center - the largest school and the fastest-growing (29 to 40, +11) - carrying dense mixture-of-experts, scaling, and reasoning threads across both cohorts, with 2025's efficiency work maturing into 2026 sparsity-for-reasoning results.",papers:[{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348"},{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.12119"},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14786"}]},{point:"The Foundation Models to Alignment link is the single densest coupling in the map (weight 1580.8), and it strengthens across years: safety and preference-alignment matured from a separate concern in 2025 into scaffolding tightly bound to frontier models in 2026.",papers:[{ay:"Qi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.05946"},{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.13708"},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20065"}]},{point:"Reasoning and test-time compute became the organizing objective, pulling RL inward as a training verb: self-correction, data recipes, and compute-scaling for reasoning dominate the 2026 wave and are the reason RL diffused into the LLM core rather than remaining separate.",papers:[{ay:"Snell 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Kumar 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.12917"},{ay:"Guha 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.04178"},{ay:"Devvrit 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13786"}]},{point:"Generative modeling grew (19 to 25, +6) and fused with the LLM stack - unified next-token-plus-diffusion, autoregressive image generation, and energy-based transformers - making Foundation Models to Generative the second-strongest coupling (1317.5).",papers:[{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039"},{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01957"},{ay:"Yu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06940"}]},{point:"Learning Theory & Optimization grew (17 to 22, +5) but reoriented toward explaining and serving the LLM stack - task-vector editing, length generalization, coverage principles, and LLM-tailored optimizers - rather than pursuing classical theory in isolation.",papers:[{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2504.10957"},{ay:"Chen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.15020"},{ay:"Amsel 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.16932"}]}],counter_evidence:[{point:"Reinforcement Learning as a standalone school shrank hardest (15 to 8, -7) and is now the smallest community (size 23): classic control, skill discovery, and open-ended agents lost Oral share even as RL-as-a-verb thrived inside LLMs, and the surviving RL papers cluster in the 2025 cohort.",papers:[{ay:"Matthews 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.23208"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.08021"},{ay:"Klissarov 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.08542"}]},{point:"Graphs, Probabilistic & Other Methods contracted the second-most (27 to 21, -6): classical probabilistic sampling, linear-RNN state tracking, and recommendation methods gave up ground as attention shifted to the LLM core.",papers:[{ay:"Grazzi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.12537"},{ay:"MOUFAD 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.09945"},{ay:"Sheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.05441"}]},{point:"The reasoning-and-evaluation boom rests on shaky measurement: training on the test task, benchmark gaming, and theoretical limits on LLM-as-judge all warn that the capability gains driving the consolidation may be partly artifacts of how they are scored.",papers:[{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07137"},{ay:"Dorner 2025",doi:null}]},{point:"Alignment gains in the dense core may be fragile: more RLHF does not straightforwardly buy more trust, fine-tuning can activate dormant adversarial behaviors, and self-improvement has provable ceilings - so the safety scaffolding binding the LLM core is less settled than its centrality suggests.",papers:[{ay:"Li 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.18870"},{ay:"Gloaguen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.16567"},{ay:"Song 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.02674"}]}],gap:"The map's single weakest seam is Learning Theory & Optimization to Reinforcement Learning (edge weight 60.7, the lowest of any pair): the field's rigor and its decision-making engine sit in separate clusters, so the RL recipes now driving frontier reasoning advance with almost no theoretical coupling.",why_it_matters:"Reading 2025 and 2026 together shows a field consolidating around scaling and governing large reasoning models - a bet that pays in capability but concentrates risk in an LLM-safety core whose theoretical and decision-theoretic foundations remain only loosely attached to it."},n=[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null,modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Studies the optimal expert sparsity of Mixture-of-Experts language models, showing that the sparsity level trading off memorization against reasoning shifts the scaling-law coefficients for reasoning tasks.",limitations:"Scaling-law coefficients are architecture- and data-pipeline-dependent, so the derived optimal sparsity may not transfer across setups.",community:0},{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Identifies 'training on the test task' as a distinct confound that inflates benchmark scores and manufactures apparent emergent abilities, separate from data contamination.",limitations:"The work mainly diagnoses and adjusts for the confound rather than offering a contamination-free evaluation standard.",community:0},{ay:"Han 2026",doi:null,modality:"vision-language",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Shows that text-only pre-trained LLMs develop latent visual priors that can be unlocked for vision tasks with only a small amount of multimodal data.",limitations:"The visual priors are characterized through correlational analysis of pre-training, so the causal mechanism remains partly inferred.",community:0},{ay:"Qi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.05946",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Argues that current LLM safety alignment is concentrated in only the first few generated tokens, making it easy to jailbreak or undo via fine-tuning, and proposes deepening alignment beyond shallow token prefixes.",limitations:"Deepening alignment is motivated and demonstrated but does not guarantee robustness against all adaptive attacks.",community:1},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20065",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes SafeDPO, folding safety constraints directly into a single Direct Preference Optimization objective to balance helpfulness and safety without complex constrained RL.",limitations:"Balancing helpfulness and safety in one objective may still require careful preference data curation to avoid over-refusal.",community:1},{ay:"Gao 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.04093",modality:"interpretability",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Develops methods to scale sparse autoencoders to large models and metrics to evaluate the interpretable features they extract from language-model activations.",limitations:"Feature quality metrics are proxies, and reconstructing activations does not by itself establish causal relevance of discovered features.",community:1},{ay:"Geffner 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00710",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces Proteina, a large-scale flow-based protein backbone generator using hierarchical fold-class conditioning for scalable de novo protein structure design.",limitations:"Generation is evaluated on backbone structure design and may still require downstream validation for sequence realizability and function.",community:2},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.18647",modality:"robotics",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Investigates whether the data scaling laws seen in language and vision also hold in imitation learning for robotic manipulation, examining how policy generalization grows with demonstration data.",limitations:"Findings are drawn from a specific manipulation setting and may not extend to all robot embodiments or tasks.",community:2},{ay:"Huang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.16196",modality:"vision-language",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Presents new insights and methods for reconstructing visual stimuli from fMRI brain recordings, improving decoding of how the brain encodes visual information.",limitations:"fMRI decoding is constrained by signal resolution and subject-specific variability, limiting generalization across individuals.",community:2},{ay:"Grazzi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.12537",modality:"theory/proof",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Shows that permitting negative eigenvalues in linear RNNs such as Mamba and DeltaNet unlocks state-tracking abilities that both Transformers and standard linear RNNs otherwise lack.",limitations:"The analysis targets state-tracking tasks specifically and does not resolve all expressivity gaps versus Transformers.",community:3},{ay:"MOUFAD 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.09945",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes a variational diffusion posterior sampling method with midpoint guidance to sample from intractable denoising posteriors when diffusion models are used as priors for Bayesian inverse problems.",limitations:"Posterior sampling still relies on approximations whose accuracy depends on the guidance scheme and the inverse problem structure.",community:3},{ay:"Thomas 2026",doi:null,modality:"optimization",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Formulates optimal multi-draft speculative sampling as a convex/optimal-transport problem, giving a principled verification-and-resampling rule that speeds LLM decoding without changing output quality.",limitations:"Gains depend on the draft model's alignment with the target model, and the convex formulation adds per-step computation.",community:3},{ay:"Yu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06940",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Shows that aligning a diffusion transformer's internal representations with pretrained self-supervised features (REPA) makes training substantially easier and improves generation quality.",limitations:"The benefit depends on the availability and quality of an external self-supervised representation to align against.",community:4},{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null,modality:"theory/proof",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces Energy-Based Transformers, a modality-agnostic energy-based framework that learns to verify and refine its own generations, enabling System-2-style inference-time computation that scales.",limitations:"Energy-based inference-time refinement can add substantial compute, and the abstract does not quantify the scaling trade-offs.",community:4},{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null,modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes Block Diffusion, interpolating between autoregressive and diffusion language models to combine parallel, controllable generation with variable-length outputs and improved likelihoods.",limitations:"Block-wise interpolation introduces a block-size design choice that mediates the trade-off between parallelism and likelihood.",community:4},{ay:"Gou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.05243",modality:"vision-language",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes universal visual grounding for GUI agents that perceive interfaces purely from pixels like humans, improving cross-platform grounding for multimodal LLM agents.",limitations:"Purely visual grounding may struggle with interface elements that are visually ambiguous without underlying accessibility metadata.",community:5},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.09967",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces Ctrl-Adapter, an efficient framework that adapts pretrained ControlNets to new image and video diffusion backbones despite feature-space mismatches, enabling versatile spatial control.",limitations:"Adapter quality is bounded by the pretrained ControlNets it reuses and the feature compatibility of the target backbone.",community:5},{ay:"Fan 2026",doi:null,modality:"vision-language",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes FlashVID, a training-free tree-based spatiotemporal token-merging scheme that compresses visual tokens to accelerate video large language models without retraining.",limitations:"Aggressive token merging risks discarding fine-grained temporal detail needed for some video-understanding tasks.",community:5},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951",modality:"theory/proof",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Formalizes LLM self-improvement as a 'sharpening' mechanism where a model refines its own generations, and analyzes the sample complexity under which such self-improvement is possible.",limitations:"The theoretical framing relies on assumptions about the model's own verification signal that may not hold for all tasks.",community:6},{ay:"Ren 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.00639",modality:"optimization",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes a recursive likelihood-ratio ('half-order') optimizer using perturbation-based gradient estimation to fine-tune the recursive chain structure of diffusion models post-training.",limitations:"Perturbation-based gradient estimators can suffer from variance that scales with the length of the diffusion chain.",community:6},{ay:"Zhuo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.15877",modality:"benchmark",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces BigCodeBench, a benchmark evaluating code generation that requires diverse function calls and following complex instructions, exposing gaps beyond simpler coding benchmarks.",limitations:"As a static benchmark it remains vulnerable to future contamination and may not capture full real-world software engineering workflows.",community:7},{ay:"Laban 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.06120",modality:"benchmark",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Shows through simulation that LLMs 'get lost' in multi-turn, underspecified conversations, with performance dropping sharply relative to fully specified single-turn tasks.",limitations:"Results rely on simulated multi-turn conversations, which may not fully reflect real user interaction patterns.",community:7},{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.11019",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Uses function vectors to characterize and then mitigate catastrophic forgetting during continual instruction tuning of large language models.",limitations:"The approach is demonstrated on instruction-tuning settings and may need adaptation for other continual-learning regimes.",community:8},{ay:"Fumero 2026",doi:null,modality:"representation learning",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Reinterprets neural models as dynamical systems over a latent space, using latent vector fields and attractors to study how representations relate to memorization and generalization.",limitations:"The dynamical-systems interpretation is primarily analytical and its practical benefits for model design remain to be established.",community:8},{ay:"Zhang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.00617",modality:"RL",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes Iterative Nash Policy Optimization, aligning LLMs with general (non-Bradley-Terry) preferences by framing RLHF as no-regret learning toward a Nash equilibrium.",limitations:"Convergence to the preference equilibrium depends on the quality and consistency of the general preference oracle.",community:9},{ay:"Zhan 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.13810",modality:"RL",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces a mean-flow policy with an instantaneous-velocity constraint that enables expressive one-step action generation, retaining flow-based policy quality while avoiding slow multi-step sampling.",limitations:"The one-step velocity constraint may limit expressiveness on highly multimodal action distributions compared with full flow sampling.",community:9}],t={eras:[{period:"2024 preprints (the shared substrate)",title:"The Common Backbone",dominant_question:"Which architectures and training recipes are worth building on?",description:"The bibliographic-coupling map is anchored by a dense core of 2024 preprints — 135 of the 437 papers carry a 2024 stamp — that both the ICLR 2025 and 2026 Oral cohorts cite in common. These are the load-bearing building blocks: Mixture-of-Experts efficiency (Jin 2024's MoE++), the diffusion-transformer representation trick (Yu 2024), the Transfusion recipe fusing next-token prediction with image diffusion (Zhou 2024), scaled sparse autoencoders for interpretability (Gao 2024), and Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks as an MLP alternative (Liu 2024). Because edges are shared references, this 2024 layer is what physically stitches the 2025 and 2026 orals into one constellation. Nearly every later debate traces back to a seed planted here.",key_papers:[{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.19756"},{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348"},{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039"},{ay:"Yu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06940"},{ay:"Gao 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.04093"}]},{period:"ICLR 2025 Orals",title:"The Evaluation Reckoning",dominant_question:"Do our benchmarks actually measure capability, or are we fooling ourselves?",description:"A striking share of the 213 ICLR 2025 orals turned a skeptical eye on measurement itself. Dominguez-Olmedo 2024 argued that training on the test task confounds both evaluation and claims of emergence; Zheng 2024 showed that null models can win automatic LLM benchmarks; and Dorner 2025 proved formal limits on LLM-as-judge evaluation. Even the vision community joined in, with Liu 2025 revisiting whether a decade of supposedly less-biased datasets is still trivially classifiable. This was less a new capability than a collective audit of the ground truth the whole field stands on.",key_papers:[{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07137"},{ay:"Dorner 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Liu 2025",doi:null}]},{period:"ICLR 2025 → 2026 (the reasoning turn)",title:"Reasoning and Self-Improvement Take Over",dominant_question:"Can models improve their own reasoning without more human labels?",description:"The strongest through-line across both conferences is reasoning. In 2025 this appeared as reinforced math instruction (Luo 2023's WizardMath), RL-based self-correction (Kumar 2024), a theory of the sharpening mechanism behind self-improvement (Huang 2024), and the claim that scaling test-time compute can beat scaling parameters (Snell 2025). By 2026 the Foundation Models school had swelled from 29 to 40 orals and the frame had shifted: Karan 2025 argues base models are already smarter than you think once you sample correctly. Reasoning stopped being a downstream fine-tuning trick and became an organizing principle for architecture and inference.",key_papers:[{ay:"Luo 2023",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2308.09583"},{ay:"Kumar 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.12917"},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Snell 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Karan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.14901"}]},{period:"ICLR 2026 Orals",title:"Foundation Models Consolidate",dominant_question:"Now that scaling works, how do we make it efficient, long-context, and cheap to serve?",description:"The 2026 cohort's center of gravity is consolidation. Foundation Models / LLMs grew to 40 orals from 29, while pure Reinforcement Learning contracted sharply from 15 to 8, its alignment work reabsorbed into preference-optimization and reasoning threads. The questions turned practical and systems-flavored: optimal MoE sparsity for reasoning (Nakamura 2026), whether MoE can beat dense models under strictly equal compute (Li 2025), the return of subquadratic backbones (Lahoti 2026's Mamba-3), lossless hierarchical speculative decoding for cheaper inference (Zhou 2026), and RL-based memory agents for long context (Yu 2026). Where 2025 asked whether scaling was even real, 2026 largely took it for granted and set about engineering it.",key_papers:[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.12119"},{ay:"Lahoti 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhou 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2601.05724"},{ay:"Yu 2026",doi:null}]},{period:"ICLR 2026 Orals",title:"The Generative Convergence",dominant_question:"Are autoregression and diffusion two methods, or one?",description:"Generative Models expanded from 19 to 25 orals, and the 2026 work increasingly refuses the old autoregressive-versus-diffusion dividing line. Arriola 2025's Block Diffusion explicitly interpolates between autoregressive and diffusion language models; Li 2026 shows a diffusion LM already knows the answer before it decodes; and Zhang 2025 ports parallel decoding into autoregressive image generation. Meanwhile the flow-matching lineage, seeded by Holderrieth 2024's Generator Matching, reframes diffusion, flow, and autoregression as instances of one Markov-process family, while Svete 2025 probes the reasoning limits of masked diffusion language models. The result is a field converging on hybrid samplers rather than picking a single generative paradigm.",key_papers:[{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01957"},{ay:"Holderrieth 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.20587"},{ay:"Svete 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13117"}]}],debates:[{name:"Scale the Transformer vs. Replace the Backbone",side_a:"Scale the transformer (dense and MoE)",side_b:"New backbones (KAN, state-space/Mamba, energy-based)",description:"One camp keeps betting on the transformer and simply makes it sparser or bigger — Nakamura 2026 tunes optimal MoE sparsity for reasoning and Li 2025 argues MoE beats dense models under strictly equal compute. The other camp proposes structurally different backbones: Liu 2024's Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks in the 2025 cohort, and in 2026 a resurgence of subquadratic sequence models with Lahoti 2026's Mamba-3, plus Gladstone 2026's Energy-Based Transformers reframing the block entirely. Rather than one side winning, 2026 mostly folds the alternatives back into the scaling story, with MoE and state-space models coexisting inside frontier recipes. The question shifted from which architecture to which mixture, at which sparsity.",status:"Tilting toward hybrid scaling; unresolved",key_papers:[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.12119"},{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.19756"},{ay:"Lahoti 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null}]},{name:"Autoregression vs. Diffusion",side_a:"Autoregressive next-token / next-patch prediction",side_b:"Diffusion / parallel denoising",description:"Zhou 2024's Transfusion already hinted the two could share one model, and the 2026 cohort pushed that blurring hard. Arriola 2025 interpolates directly between autoregressive and diffusion language models, while Li 2026 finds diffusion LMs converge on the answer before decoding and Zhang 2025 imports parallel decoding into autoregressive image generation. Generative Models grew from 19 to 25 orals largely on this convergence, so the 2025 which-paradigm-wins framing softened into how to interpolate between them. The tension persists over likelihood modeling and controllability — Svete 2025 still interrogates what masked diffusion can and cannot reason about — but the hard boundary is gone.",status:"Converging / blurring",key_papers:[{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039"},{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01957"},{ay:"Svete 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13117"}]},{name:"Reinforcement Learning vs. Direct Preference Optimization",side_a:"RL from rewards (RLHF, RLVR, trust regions)",side_b:"Direct preference optimization (the DPO family)",description:"For aligning and improving reasoning models, one side runs full RL — Zhang 2024's iterative Nash policy optimization, Becker 2025's trust-region RLVR — while the other collapses it into direct preference losses like Yang 2025's token-importance DPO. The balance is genuinely contested: Gopalan 2025 argues DPO is a misspecified estimator even as DPO variants multiply, and Agrawal 2025 claims reflective prompt evolution can outperform reinforcement learning entirely. Structurally, the standalone Reinforcement Learning school shrank from 15 to 8 orals between 2025 and 2026, not because RL lost but because its alignment work dispersed into the Foundation-Models and preference-optimization literature. The method wars stayed unresolved; the org chart just redrew around them.",status:"Unresolved; RL work dispersing into preference optimization",key_papers:[{ay:"Zhang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.00617"},{ay:"Yang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.19653"},{ay:"Gopalan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20413"},{ay:"Becker 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817"},{ay:"Agrawal 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.19457"}]},{name:"Sparse Autoencoders vs. Causal Circuits",side_a:"Dictionary learning / sparse autoencoders",side_b:"Circuits and causal attribution graphs",description:"Interpretability splits over what the right unit of explanation is. The dictionary-learning camp scales sparse autoencoders to pull interpretable features out of activations (Gao 2024, extended by Bhalla 2025's temporal SAEs), while the circuits camp traces causal subnetworks and computation graphs (Marks 2024's sparse feature circuits, then Zhao 2025 verifying chain-of-thought through its computational graph). The Alignment, Safety & Interpretability school held almost flat at 31 then 30 orals, but its internal emphasis moved in 2026 toward causal verification of reasoning and even critiques of the intervention methods themselves (Grant 2025). Neither approach has subsumed the other; they increasingly get combined.",status:"Both advancing; converging on causal verification",key_papers:[{ay:"Gao 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.04093"},{ay:"Marks 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2403.19647"},{ay:"Bhalla 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2511.05541"},{ay:"Zhao 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.09312"},{ay:"Grant 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2511.04638"}]},{name:"Are Benchmarks Measuring Real Capability?",side_a:"Benchmarks are confounded and gameable",side_b:"Build harder, dynamic, agentic evaluations",description:"The skeptics showed the ground is soft: Dominguez-Olmedo 2024 tied apparent emergence to training on the test task, Zheng 2024 won benchmarks with null models, and Dorner 2025 proved LLM-judges cannot out-scale twice the data. The builders answered in 2026 not by defending old benchmarks but by making evaluation adversarial and dynamic — Froger 2026's Gaia2 for asynchronous agents and Zhang 2025's train-before-test protocol to harmonize model rankings. Datasets & Benchmarks grew modestly from 16 to 18 orals, and the center of gravity moved from static leaderboards toward simulated environments. The critique was largely accepted; the response is better instruments, not denial.",status:"Critique accepted; shifting to dynamic/agentic evaluation",key_papers:[{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07137"},{ay:"Dorner 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Froger 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.05195"}]},{name:"Scale Inference vs. Scale Pretraining",side_a:"Spend compute at test time (thinking, sampling, verification)",side_b:"Spend compute on pretraining and data",description:"Snell 2025 opened the 2025 case that optimally scaling test-time compute can beat adding parameters, and Huang 2024 gave a theory for the self-generated sharpening that makes it work. The 2026 cohort complicated the dichotomy from both ends: Karan 2025 shows careful sampling already unlocks latent reasoning in base models, while Chen 2025's coverage principle formalizes how pre-training is exactly what enables effective post-training and test-time search. Kim 2025's study of pre-training under near-infinite compute pushes the same coupling, and Learning Theory & Optimization grew from 17 to 22 orals partly on these tradeoffs. The emerging answer is both, coupled — pre-training sets the ceiling that test-time search then reaches.",status:"Reframed as complementary rather than either/or",key_papers:[{ay:"Snell 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Karan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.14901"},{ay:"Chen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.15020"},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14786"}]}]},o=`## Two Cohorts, One Sky

Placing all 437 Oral papers — 213 from ICLR 2025 and 224 from 2026 — on a single map, and drawing edges from shared references and content similarity rather than submission date, does something a year-by-year view cannot: it makes the boundary between the cohorts disappear. Papers do not cluster by conference; they cluster by what they cite. Wherever a 2026 oral and a 2025 oral descend from the same prior work, they settle side by side, and the two years interleave into continuous threads. The ten official topic-schools are real but porous — modularity is low, an intellectual continuum more than an archipelago of separate fields. Read together, the snapshots become a short film, and the plot is mostly about consolidation.

### The Center of Gravity

The densest coupling anywhere on the map is between **Foundation Models / LLMs** and **Alignment, Safety & Interpretability** — stronger than any other school pair in either year. Together the two schools account for roughly a third of all 437 orals, and they hold that dominance across both cohorts: Alignment stayed level (31→30) while Foundation Models grew hardest of all (29→40). What binds them is a shared reference spine in preference optimization and RLHF. On the safety side sit **Qi 2024** ("Safety Alignment Should Be Made More Than Just a Few Tokens Deep") and **Kim 2025** (SafeDPO); on the capability side, **Yang 2025** (Token-Importance Guided DPO) and **Luo 2023** (WizardMath) — all drinking from the same well. Interpretability supplies the field's most-cited safety anchors, **Gao 2024** (scaling sparse autoencoders) and **Marks 2024** (sparse feature circuits), while the highest-connectivity hubs — **Nakamura 2026** (optimal MoE sparsity), **Jin 2024** (MoE++) — mark the foundation-model core itself.

### What Grew, What Faded

The growth is concentrated. **Foundation Models** added the most (+11), pushed by mixture-of-experts and sub-quadratic sequence modeling: **Li 2025** (MoE can surpass dense LLMs), **Lahoti 2026** (Mamba-3), **Malach 2026** (tool-use unlocking length generalization in state-space models). **Generative Models** rose next (+6), toward autoregressive image and energy-based generation — **Gladstone 2026** (Energy-Based Transformers, the single most connected node on the map), **Zhang 2025** (locality-aware parallel decoding), **Go 2025** (text-to-3D). **Learning Theory & Optimization** grew quietly (+5) with **Ren 2025** and **Nguyen 2025**.

The two retreats tell a subtler story. **Reinforcement Learning** shrank most sharply (15→8) and **Graphs, Probabilistic & Other Methods** fell close behind (27→21). But RL did not leave the field — it changed address. The classic RL orals — **Matthews 2024** (Kinetix), **Klissarov 2024** (MaestroMotif), **Zhang 2024** (Iterative Nash Policy Optimization) — thinned, while reinforcement learning as a *method* migrated wholesale into the LLM cluster: **Becker 2025** (TROLL, trust regions for RL on LLMs), **Wu 2026** (LongWriter-Zero), **Devvrit 2025** (scaling RL compute for LLMs), **Wang 2025** (LoongRL). The RL school contracted because its center of mass relocated to Foundation Models.

### Bridges Across the Year Line

The papers the map routes its traffic through — its highest-betweenness nodes — are precisely the boundary-crossers, and several are 2026 works fastening the new cohort onto the old. **Zhou 2024**'s Transfusion, which trains one transformer on next-token prediction *and* diffusion, sits on the Foundation ↔ Generative seam and is one of the busiest junctions on the map. **Ying 2025** (Exploratory Diffusion for Unsupervised RL) and **Peng 2026** (Planner-Aware Path Learning in Diffusion Language Models) each fuse generative diffusion with, respectively, reinforcement learning and language modeling — exactly the crossings the strongest school-pair links reward. Most fitting of all, **Wu 2025**'s "LLM DNA: Tracing Model Evolution via Functional Representations" is a top bridge whose very subject is lineage across model generations: the map using a paper about tracing ancestry to stitch its own two years together.

### The Gap

Not everything connects. The single weakest coupling anywhere on the map is **Learning Theory & Optimization ↔ Reinforcement Learning** — the two schools that were once a single conversation now barely share a citation. The 2025–26 RL orals are empirical and increasingly LLM-flavored (Nash policy optimization, visual RL), while the theory orals cluster around optimization and generalization (**Ren 2025**'s half-order fine-tuning, **Nguyen 2025**'s subspace training). Both schools are small, both moved little, and they cite past one another. Learning Theory is nearly as distant from Datasets & Benchmarks, making it the map's most self-contained neighborhood — rigorous, and somewhat alone.

### The Through-Line

Read forward, the arc is legible. The 2025 cohort's most-cited orals were architectural landmarks — **Liu 2024** (KAN), **Ravi 2024** (SAM 2) — general-purpose primitives offered to everyone. The 2026 hubs are refinements of the stack those primitives now feed: sparsity, decoding, test-time compute. Through both years the center held, a Foundation ↔ Alignment core where capability and safety draw on one reference list, absorbing reinforcement learning as a tool even as the standalone RL school emptied out. The densest edges point where the field is already heading: generation and language modeling continuing to merge — Transfusion, energy-based transformers, diffusion language models — around a foundation-model core, with the theory of it all trailing a half-step behind the systems it hopes to explain.`,r={schools:e,gap:i,argument_spine:a,evidence:n,debate:t,review_markdown:o};export{a as argument_spine,t as debate,r as default,n as evidence,i as gap,o as review_markdown,e as schools};
